module

public import Sheaf.CategoryTheory.Sites.GrothendieckTopos
public import Mathlib.CategoryTheory.Sites.Descent.IsStack
public import Mathlib.CategoryTheory.Bicategory.Modification.Pseudo
public import Mathlib.CategoryTheory.Groupoid

/-! # Local essential surjectivity of a fully faithful morphism of stacks

Source: Stacks Project, stacks.tex, Tag 046N
(blueprint: lem-sfb-stack-fully-faithful-local-equivalence).

As in `GroupoidStackificationLocalDescription`, a fibred groupoid with coherent
choices of pullback is encoded by a pseudofunctor on the opposite base. Strong
transformations encode cartesian functors over the base, and invertible modifications
encode natural isomorphisms over its identity. The universe `w` is the larger universe
of `def-weil-size-convention`; the site and its fibre groupoids are small there.
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe w

open CategoryTheory Opposite

namespace CategoryTheory.Pseudofunctor

set_option backward.isDefEq.respectTransparency false in
/-- A fibrewise fully faithful morphism between stacks in groupoids is an equivalence
over the base exactly when every target object is locally in its essential image.

Source: Stacks Project, stacks.tex, Tag 046N
(blueprint: lem-sfb-stack-fully-faithful-local-equivalence).

`IsStack` asserts that the slice morphism presheaves are sheaves and every covering
object descent datum is effective. Since every fibre is a groupoid, morphisms here
are precisely isomorphisms. A covering sieve is used instead of an indexed family:
its arrows form a covering family, and the sieve generated by any covering family
has the same local-image property, by further pullback. No fibre products are assumed.

Proof: (1) an inverse and its counit give a global preimage of every target object;
the maximal sieve therefore witnesses the local-image property. (2) Conversely,
choose local source objects and isomorphisms from their images to the restrictions
of a fixed target object. On every common test object over two covering arrows,
full faithfulness lifts the comparison of these isomorphisms to a unique source
isomorphism. Uniqueness gives compatibility with restrictions, identity comparisons,
and the cocycle equation on common triple tests. All comparisons include the
pseudofunctor composition constraints and the naturality constraints of `F`.
(3) Effective object descent in the source glues the resulting descent datum to
a source object. (4) Its image is locally isomorphic to the fixed target object;
the cocycle equation makes these isomorphisms compatible, so the target's slice
isomorphism sheaf glues them uniquely to a global isomorphism. This is the sheaf
axiom of `def-weil-pbc-topos`. Empty covering sieves are allowed: effectivity still
supplies the source object and unique gluing still supplies the isomorphism.
(5) The fibre functors are now fully faithful and essentially surjective. Apply
`lem-sfb-fibred-groupoid-equivalence-criterion`, with sizes as in
`def-weil-size-convention`, to obtain a cartesian inverse and invertible unit and
counit over identities, expressed here by strong transformations and modifications. -/
theorem stack_equivalence_iff_locally_essentiallySurjective_of_fullyFaithful
    {C : Type w} [SmallCategory C] (J : GrothendieckTopology C)
    (A B : Pseudofunctor (LocallyDiscrete Cᵒᵖ) Cat.{w, w})
    (hA : ∀ T : C, IsGroupoid (A.obj (.mk (op T))))
    (hB : ∀ T : C, IsGroupoid (B.obj (.mk (op T))))
    [A.IsStack J] [B.IsStack J] (F : StrongTrans A B)
    (hF : ∀ T : C, Nonempty (F.app (.mk (op T))).toFunctor.FullyFaithful) :
    (∃ G : StrongTrans B A,
      Nonempty (@CategoryTheory.Iso (StrongTrans A A)
        (StrongTrans.homCategory (F := A) (G := A))
        (StrongTrans.vcomp F G) (StrongTrans.id A)) ∧
      Nonempty (@CategoryTheory.Iso (StrongTrans B B)
        (StrongTrans.homCategory (F := B) (G := B))
        (StrongTrans.vcomp G F) (StrongTrans.id B))) ↔
    ∀ (T : C) (b : B.obj (.mk (op T))),
      ∃ R : Sieve T, R ∈ J T ∧
        ∀ (V : C) (f : V ⟶ T), R f →
          ∃ a : A.obj (.mk (op V)),
            Nonempty ((F.app (.mk (op V))).toFunctor.obj a ≅
              (B.map f.op.toLoc).toFunctor.obj b) := by
  sorry

end CategoryTheory.Pseudofunctor
